# Defining words

## Colon definitions

```forth
: name ( stack -- effect )
  ... ;
```

`;` ends the definition. Use `recursive` (Gforth) or `recurse` when the word calls itself — see `forth-control.mdc`.

## CREATE / DOES>

Two stack effects: **calling the defining word**, and **calling each created word** (after `does>`).

```forth
: cell-array ( n "name" -- )
  create cells allot
  does> ( i a-addr -- addr )
    swap cells + ;

: constant ( n "name" -- )
  create ,
  does> ( -- n )
    @ ;
```

The stack comment at `does>` describes the **created word's** effect; the body receives the data address implicitly.

## Field offsets

Factor repeated `( addr ) offset +` into a defining word:

```forth
: simple-field ( n "name" -- )
  create ,
  does> ( a-addr -- a-addr+n )
    @ + ;
```

Access raw body without `does>` behaviour: `' word >body`.

### +field / end-struct (portable ANS)

Pattern from `compat/struct.fs` (theForthNet) — stack `( align offset -- align' offset' )` while declaring:

```forth
: +field ( size offset "name" -- offset' )
  create over , +
  does> ( addr1 -- addr2 )  @ + ;

\ Offsets while declaring (size offset -- offset'):
1 cells 0 +field l-value
1 cells 1 cells + +field l-next
create node1  2 cells allot
```

`l-next` converts `( node-addr -- next-field-addr )`. Prefer Gforth `begin-structure` when available — see `forth-dialect-gforth.mdc`.

### Header + inline buffer (queue pattern)

Defining word with **fixed header cells** then `allot` for data (priority-queue idiom):

```forth
: Queue: ( max "name" -- )
  create  , 0 ,  cells allot ;   \ max, count=0, item slots

: q@count ( q -- u )  cell+ @ ;
: q@data ( q -- addr )  2 cells + ;

: q! ( n q -- )
  dup q@count tuck 1+ rot q@data swap cells + !  cell+ 1 swap ! ;
```

Use named throw constants for underflow/overflow instead of silent failure — see `forth-memory.mdc` for list/queue error handling.

## value / to

```forth
: value ( n "name" -- )
  create ,
  does> ( -- n )  @ ;
: to ( n "name" -- )
  ' >body ! ;

5 value speed   7 to speed
```

## defer / is

Indirect execution — useful for hooks and indirect recursion:

```forth
defer action
: default-action ( -- ) ." default" ;
' default-action is action
```

## Quotations and anonymous words

```forth
: warn ( n -- )
  [: cr ." warn " . ;] stderr outfile-execute ;

Defer deferred
:noname ( -- ) ... ;          \ ANS: leaves xt on stack
latestxt is deferred          \ Gforth: noname + latestxt
```

Use `[: … ;]` when passing an xt to `catch`, `outfile-execute`, etc. **`include forward.fs`** + **`Forward name`** for mutual recursion without `defer` overhead (colon defs only).

## Aliases

```forth
' then alias endif            \ same xt, new name
' foo alias bar immediate     \ immediate flag is per-alias
```

## Arrays and structures

Do not over-build array definers — often `create n cells allot` plus `@`/`!` and `cell+` is enough.

Gforth `struct` / `begin-structure` — see `forth-dialect-gforth.mdc`. Allocation idioms:

```forth
list% %allot              \ dictionary, leaves addr
list% %alloc throw        \ heap via allocate
list% %size .             \ byte size of one instance
```

Field words convert `( struct-addr -- field-addr )`. Memory alignment — see `forth-memory.mdc`.

## Literals and postpone

`[ … ] literal`, `sliteral`, `postpone`, `'`, `[']` — see `forth-meta.mdc`.

## VOCABULARY / search order

Avoid search-order tricks in portable code unless the project uses them — see `forth-wordlists.mdc`.

## Parameter stack as array

Prefer real buffers over stack indexing — see `forth-anti-patterns.mdc`.
